// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HYBRID WRITEUP DRAFT — prose for judges, VSM blocks as evidence.
// Export: strip leading "// " for Kaggle markdown; VSM blocks stay.
// Track: ARC-AGI-3. Budget: ~1300 prose words. P-1 pending: notebook.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// TITLE: Learning to See: A Verify-Then-Route Loop for ARC
// SUBTITLE: Small rule sets, honest detectors, milestones that transfer
// from static puzzles to interactive games. Track: ARC-AGI-3.

// INTRO. ARC-AGI-2 gives pairs; ARC-AGI-3 gives a grid, actions, and
// silence until a level completes. We treat both as one problem:
// RECOGNIZE what the situation is about, then act from a verified
// toolbox. A recognizer classifies dynamics from observations; detectors
// prove each rule reproduces training data (leave-one-out) before it may
// touch anything unseen; a router tries the single predicted strategy
// first. Every claim below is measured, including the zeros.

// METHOD I (static). Classify-then-route over train pairs: detectors for
// recolor, tiling, symmetry, movement, fill and twelve more dynamics.
// A memorizing nearest-neighbor that passed naive checks was caught by
// leave-one-out and excluded — the gate works. Routing reorders a fixed
// chain, so it cannot regress by construction (zero regressions to date).
// One detector, written exactly as it runs:

task("d_sizefill") = {
  body: "hollow interiors fill with color keyed by size; dots preserved",
  verify: "leave-one-out over train pairs",
  precision: "3/4 full-1000 (miss 868de0fa recorded, not hidden)",
}

// METHOD II (interactive). The same loop online: a fixed probe suite maps
// every action to its effect; grids embed as R^23 vectors so runs become
// trajectories; MILESTONES are displacements above 5x the run median,
// which solves sparse reward without per-game tuning; visited-state
// hashing plus dead-click memory never re-try the void. A second loop
// hunts autonomously (cluster ranking, depth-2 composer, 3,258 dead-ends
// recorded so no failure repeats); it found nothing, published as data.
// Human play plugs into the same interface: a 2/6-level run dissected
// into probe/transform/check became a game branch verbatim.

// RESULTS. ARC-AGI-2 (1000 training): fixed-order blind 1.3%, routed
// 3.9% (precision@1 3.8%), toolbox oracle 4.4%, 20 dynamics with 19
// solving, ~8 tries of 30, one minute per full run, bit-identical across
// re-pulls. ARC-AGI-3 (25 local games): first levels via button
// repetition (5-click threshold, position-gated) and a human-derived
// bulk+check cycle; a 5-game probe library plus adaptive milestones
// separate cursor noise from bulk transforms with no per-game tuning.
// Scorecards published per run. Full table, live:

report("full-1000") = {
  routed: "39 (3.9%)",
  oracle: "44 (4.4%)",
  regressions: 0,
  reproducibility: "re-pulled data, identical numbers",
}

// THEORY. Verification-before-routing bounds overfitting by construction:
// a rule cannot fire where it cannot reproduce. Milestones are shaped
// reward with a derivation (displacement bands separate micro/medium/
// large events across games). Failure is first-class data: lockouts (an
// early check voids a level forever), viability complements (dead-ends
// map exactly where the system must not go), family fragmentation
// (extract/draw/split each split on measurement). The observer
// (reporter, milestones, dead-ends) is part of the loop it improves.

// UNIVERSALITY + PROGRESS. One taxonomy drives both tracks
// (move/paint/transform map to cursor/interact/bulk); the category
// catalog maintains itself as new dynamics solve. Everything open:
// code, reports, negatives. Reproduce our numbers or extend the toolbox;
// each addition costs one detector plus one measurement.

// CLOSE. Small verified loops beat large unverified ones. Modest numbers,
// auditable end to end — and every zero taught us where the next point
// lives. Links: repository, notebooks, scorecards (at submit time).

